	abstract = {This study explores the use of Model Context Protocol (MCP) in generating CHED-based college lesson plans by comparing Zero-Shot Baseline prompting, CHED-MCP Assisted generation, and Zero-Shot Iterative refinement workflows. The findings show that MCP-assisted generation requires more input tokens because it retrieves curriculumrelated resources before generation, while output token counts remain similar to Zero-Shot prompting. The iterative workflow improved lesson-plan quality but significantly increased cumulative token costs due to repeated refinement prompts. Overall, the study argues that token efficiency should not be measured solely by token reduction, but as a workflow-level and quality-adjusted concept that considers curriculum alignment, evidence grounding, completeness, and teacher usability.  © 2026 IEEE.},
