	abstract = {A large number of Linux kernel device drivers are tightly coupled with hardware, making their execution and testing heavily dependent on device interfaces and responses. Without access to real hardware or accurate device emulation, significant parts of driver code remain untested, posing a major risk to kernel security. Existing approaches mainly rely on manual device modeling or limited physical hardware, making it difficult to scale across the vast driver ecosystem. In this paper, we introduce DevGen, the first approach for automatically generating virtual devices using Large Language Models (LLMs) to enhance kernel driver fuzzing. Our approach extracts hardware interaction logic from driver source code and generates QEMU-based virtual devices iteratively, employing syntax checking and semantic repair guided by execution feedback. Furthermore, the generated devices are integrated with Syzkaller, enabling the exploration of driver code paths without the need for physical hardware. Experiments on 50 drivers from the latest Linux 6.18 kernel show that DevGen successfully generates virtual devices for 44 drivers. With these generated models, 24% of the drivers achieve significant improvements in fuzzing coverage, and 7 previously unknown crashes are triggered. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
