	doi = {10.1145/3810280.3810329},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105048784636?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Code auditing plays a pivotal role in safeguarding the software supply chain. Existing vulnerability detection methods in code auditing generally adopt a combination of traditional static analysis, dynamic analysis tools, and manual review. However, this model faces a fundamental bottleneck in balancing auditing efficiency, code scale, and detection generality, which seriously limits its effectiveness in complex software scenarios. The rapid rise of Large Language Models (LLMs) provides an important technical opportunity to overcome this bottleneck. Nevertheless, in practical code auditing scenarios, LLMs are often constrained by issues such as insufficient semantic information in short contexts and hallucinations induced by long contexts, making it difficult for auditing accuracy and result reliability to meet practical requirements. To address these challenges, this study proposes a Function and Variable level dual-context-enhanced Retrieval and Slicing (FV-RAG) technique, which accurately extracts vulnerability-related, semantically enhanced contexts and effectively compensates for the limitations of LLMs in deep semantic understanding of code. Based on this technique, a general code auditing agent framework integrating LLMs and program analysis technologies is designed and implemented, enabling full automation of the vulnerability auditing and detection process. Based on this framework, instantiation development and preliminary validation are conducted for typical buffer overflow vulnerabilities in C/C++ code. In addition, two sets of comparative experiments are designed. Using a specialized dataset of buffer overflow vulnerabilities in C/C++ code, the experiments verify both the performance improvement brought by the vulnerability-related semantic contexts extracted by the FV-RAG technique and the feasibility and operational stability of the proposed code auditing agent framework when adapted to different LLMs. The research results not only provide an efficient and general technical paradigm for large-scale code auditing at the source code repository level, but also lay a solid theoretical and technical foundation for the reliable application of deep integration between LLMs and program analysis technologies in the field of security auditing.  © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
