	url = {https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/105050170850?origin=resultslist},
	abstract = {Strategic consulting evaluation in complex geopolitical and security environments demands analytical depth, cross-domain generalization, and reliable uncertainty quantification - capabilities that existing approaches largely fail to deliver. In particular, prior methods suffer from three critical limitations: (1) shallow single-turn reasoning that cannot handle multi-step strategic inference chains, (2) domain mismatch caused by generic retrieval mechanisms that ignore task-specific contextual requirements, and (3) poorly calibrated confidence estimates that undermine decision-maker trust in automated assessments. To address these challenges, we propose LLM-SCE, a Large Language Model-driven Strategic Consulting Evaluation framework integrating three synergistic innovations: (1) a hierarchical Multi-Agent reasoning architecture that decomposes complex queries into structured analytical sub-tasks, (2) a Domain-Adaptive retrieval module that dynamically aligns retrieved context with task-specific strategic dimensions, and (3) a Chain-of-Thought prompting mechanism coupled with temperature- calibrated confidence scoring. Extensive experiments across four strategic task types - political risk, economic assessment, military analysis, and diplomatic consulting - demonstrate that LLM-SCE consistently outperforms strong baselines, achieving up to 8.4% improvement in F1 score while maintaining superior robustness and calibration. © 2026 Copyright held by the owner/author(s).},
