	abstract = {Industrial Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) systems serving heterogeneous multimodal document repositories, such as the Maintainability Design Audit (MDA) corpora targeted in this work, routinely combine multiple retrievers, and the choice of fusion strategy directly governs retrieval accuracy and downstream answer correctness. However, existing RAG techniques lack a unified theoretical framework for multi-retriever fusion, and fixed-weight strategies (Reciprocal Rank Fusion, linear interpolation) fail to adapt to diverse corpus characteristics. This paper proposes MoRE-RAG (Mixture-of-Retrieval-Experts RAG), which introduces Bayesian decision theory into the RAG fusion mechanism. We derive a closed-form optimal weight for arbitrary N retrieval experts that unifies retrieval-layer and modality-layer fusion under a single mathematical framework. On top of this formulation, we integrate hierarchical mixed retrieval and entity confidence gating to form a RAG framework tailored for industrial multimodal documents. Experiments on four BEIR benchmark datasets and industrial maintenance corpora demonstrate that MoRE-Ensemble achieves an 18.82% average improvement in NDCG@10 over RRF (p<0.001), while end-to-end generation on the industrial corpus surpasses all baselines on Answer Correctness, Context Relevancy, and F1. Ablation studies and weight analyses confirm the effectiveness of each component and the adaptive properties of Bayesian weights. A labelling-budget analysis further shows that 50–200 paired labels suffice to obtain stable fusion weights, making the framework practical for industrial deployment under limited annotation resources. © 2026 Elsevier B.V.},
